{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::0","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Lecture 2  Boolean\nAlgebra","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":1,"pageEnd":1,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::1","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"CIS220","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"CIS220\nWhat did we go over last week?\n● Course Policies\n● Exam schedule\n● Quiz schedule\n● Assignments\n● Introduction to Logic\n● Truth Tables\n● Course Overview\nLogical Conjunctions\n● Or\n● And\n● Not\nAll three of these logical Operators can be derived from every computer\nprogram\nHow do we turn our linguistic objectives into logistic answers and\nconditions?\np = years of service\nq = bacholors\nI want a candidate to have 15 years of experience and a bachelor's\n→ I want a candidate to have 15 years of experience and a bachelor's\np = 15 years of experience\nq = has a bachelor's\nDoes the candidate have 15 years of experience?\n- Yes\n- No\nDoes the candidate have a bachelorʼs?\n- Yes\n- No\nif p and q are true, then itʼs true that I want to hire them.\np ^ q = 1\nThis is boolean algebra\n● Is the algebra of logic\n● Developed by George Boole in 1854\n● Works with two logical values\n○ True\n○ False\n● Uses logical operators (logical conjunctions)","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":1,"pageEnd":8,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::2","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"○ AND","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"○ AND","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":8,"pageEnd":8,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::3","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"○ OR","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"○ OR","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":8,"pageEnd":8,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::4","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"○ NOT","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"○ NOT\n○ Conditional\n○ Biconditional\n○ Exclusive OR\n● Boolean algebra lets us change the form of a logical expression without\nchanging its meaning.\nWhat is Boolean Algebra?\nBoolean Operators\n● AND: p ∧ q\n● OR: p ∨ q\n● Not: ¬p\nHow do we represent them?\np → q\nIf Statements in Boolean Algebra\np q p → q","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":8,"pageEnd":10,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::5","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"F F T","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"F F T","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":10,"pageEnd":10,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::6","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"F T T","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"F T T\nT F\nF","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":10,"pageEnd":10,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::7","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"T T T","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"T T T\nExample\nLet:\np = “It is Tuesdayˮ\nq = “I will be at the partyˮ\nThen:\np → q\nMeans:\n“If it is Tuesday, then I will be at the\nparty.ˮ\nThe only way this promise is false is\nif it is Tuesday and I do not show up.\nLet:\nq = x\np = y\nThen:\nq ∧ p= x AND y\nq ∨ p = x OR x\n¬q = not x\n“Let-then-meansˮ frame work:\nMeans:\nIt is x and y\nIts is x or x\nThis is not x\nExample\nLet:\np = \"It is raining\"\nq = \"It is cold\"\nThen:\np ∧ q = \"It is raining AND it is cold\"\np ∨ q = \"It is raining OR it is cold\"\n¬p = \"It is NOT raining\"\nLogical Equivalence","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":10,"pageEnd":14,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::8","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"P ≡ Q","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"P ≡ Q\n● P and Q always have the same truth value\n● They can look different\n● They still represent the same logical result\nExample\n¬(¬p) ≡ p\n● If we say:\n○ It is not true that it is not raining\n● This has the same logical meaning as:\n○ It is raining\nDouble Negation Law\n¬(¬p) ≡ p\n● Applying NOT twice returns the original value\n● Similar to saying \"not not\"\np ¬p ¬(¬p)","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":14,"pageEnd":16,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::9","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"T F T","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"T F T","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":16,"pageEnd":16,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf::10","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"lecture 2 logic lecture 2 logic","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures/Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","fileName":"Lecture 2 - Logic.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Foundations of CS Lectures","title":"Lecture 2 - Logic","heading":"F T F","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"F T F\nLaw of Excluded Middle\np ∨ ¬p ≡ T\nA proposition must be either true or false.\nExample:\nThe computer is on OR the computer is not on.\nOne of these must be true.\nLaw of Contradiction\np ∧ ¬p ≡ F\nA proposition and its negation cannot both be true.\nExample:\nThe computer is on AND the computer is not on.\nThis cannot be true.\nIdentity Law\nIdentity Laws\nT ∧ p ≡ p\nF ∨ p ≡ p\nT  True\nF  False\nAdding a value that cannot change the result leaves p unchanged.\nExample: T ∧ p ≡ p\nLet:\np = \"The computer is connected to the\nInternet.\"\nThen:\nT ∧ p\nMeans:\n\"True AND the computer is\nconnected to the Internet.\"\nSince T is already true, the\nresult depends entirely on p.\nIdempotent Law\np ∧ p ≡ p\np ∨ p ≡ p\nRepeating the same proposition does not change its value.\nExample:\nIt is raining AND it is raining.\nCommutative Laws\np ∧ q ≡ q ∧ p\np ∨ q ≡ q ∨ p\nThe order of propositions does not change the result.\nExample\nIt is raining AND it is cold.\nIt is cold AND it is raining.\nIt is raining OR it is cold.\nIt is cold OR it is raining.\nAssociative Laws\n(p ∧ q) ∧ r ≡ p ∧ (q ∧ r)\n(p ∨ q) ∨ r ≡ p ∨ (q ∨ r)\nChanging the grouping does not change the result\nUseful when working with several AND or OR operations\nInstead of worrying about:\n(p ∧ q) ∧ r\nor\np ∧ (q ∧ r)\nwe can often simply think of:\np ∧ q ∧ r\np ∧ (q ∨ r) ≡ (p ∧ q) ∨ (p ∧ r)\nand\np ∨ (q ∧ r) ≡ (p ∨ q) ∧ (p ∨ r)\nDistributive Laws\nLet:\na = \"This card is an ace\"\ns = \"This card is a spade\"\nc = \"This card is a club\"\nThen:\na ∧ (s ∨ c)\nMeans:\nThis card is an ace and is either a\nspade or a club.\nThis is equivalent to:\n(a ∧ s) ∨ (a ∧ c)\nThis card is the ace of spades OR the\nace of clubs.\n¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q\n¬(p ∨ q) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q\nEasy Rule to Remember:\nWhen NOT moves inside\n→ AND becomes OR\n→ OR becomes AND\nEach proposition is negated\nDeMorgan's Laws\nStatement:\nA raven is large and black.\nLet:\np = \"The bird is large\"\nq = \"The bird is black\"\nThen:\np ∧ q\nMeans:\nThe bird is large AND black.\nWhat does ¬(p ∧ q) Mean?\nThe bird could be:\n● Not large\n● Not black\n● Not large and not black\nTherefore:\n¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q\nRecall:\np → q ≡ ¬p ∨ q\nTherefore:\n¬(p → q)\nbecomes:\n¬(¬p ∨ q)\nNegating an \"If\" Statement\nUsing DeMorgan's Law:\n¬(¬p) ∧ ¬q\nUsing Double Negation:\np ∧ ¬q\nFinal Result\n¬(p → q) ≡ p ∧ ¬q\nSimplifying\nBoolean algebra allows us to simplify\nexpressions step by step.\nStart:\np ∧ (p → q)\nReplace the implication:\np ∧ (¬p ∨ q) These are logically\nequivalent: p → q ≡ ¬p ∨ q)\nDistribute:\n(p ∧ ¬p) ∨ (p ∧ q)\nContradiction:\nF ∨ (p ∧ q)\nIdentity:\np ∧ q\nResult\np ∧ (p → q) ≡ p ∧ q","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":16,"pageEnd":32,"embeddingF32":""}]}